rejected.instruction:
You are evaluating the clinical note against the consultation transcript it was generated from.

Your goal is correct ordering, not general harshness. A faithful, clinically complete note should score above a note that adds, changes, or omits established clinical content. Do not penalise style, brevity, formatting, or ordinary clinical summarisation; do penalise differences that would change what a later clinician understands was found, decided, prescribed, advised, excluded, or safety-netted.

Judge only against what the transcript establishes. Do not require ideal care that was not discussed. Do not invent missing items from clinical expectations. If the transcript is unclear, uncertain, or inaudible, the note must not turn that uncertainty into a precise drug, value, diagnosis, result, dose, or instruction unless it is established elsewhere in the transcript.

Internally compare in this order:

1. Build a checklist of load-bearing transcript facts:
- presenting problem, onset, duration, progression, severity, laterality, relevant context and exposures;
- important positives and pertinent negatives, especially red flags and negatives used to justify triage or differential diagnosis;
- working diagnosis, differential diagnosis, diagnostic uncertainty, and rationale;
- past history, current medication, pregnancy status where relevant, and drug allergies/intolerances;
- examination findings, observations, and test results only if actually performed, reported, or available;
- management plan: prescriptions, medication names, doses, frequency, duration, route, whether actually prescribed versus only suggested/conditional;
- investigations, referrals, review plans, follow-up timing, urgency, thresholds for escalation, and safety-net advice;
- work/activity restrictions, infection-control/public-health advice, and other practical instructions when they affect safety or timing of care;
- discrete health-relevant background such as smoking, alcohol, exercise, occupation, family history, and social context, while treating low-impact omissions as peripheral.

2. Compare the note to that checklist:
- Accept paraphrase, standard abbreviations, conventional clinical wording, and recording a fact once rather than repeating it.
- Accept umbrella phrases only when they preserve the same clinical meaning. For example, a standard synonym such as “dysuria” can cover “pain when passing urine”; but a generic phrase such as “safety-netted”, “red flags discussed”, “needs review”, “bloods arranged”, or “consider antibiotics” does not preserve specific triggers, timing, urgency, conditions, or constraints if those were stated in the transcript.
- Do not split semantically identical repetitions into multiple omissions.
- Do not penalise omitted conversational colour, reassurance, or administrative small talk unless it carries clinical or safety meaning.
- If a note says an examination was performed, gives normal examination findings, states test results, or supplies exact medication instructions, those details must be supported by the transcript.

Pay particular attention to partial omissions. Do not give full credit merely because the general topic appears somewhere in the note. Locate the exact surviving wording and ask whether it preserves the load-bearing details:
- polarity: present, absent, denied, uncertain, worsening, improving;
- subject: patient versus relative, clinician advice versus patient belief;
- status: actual versus possible, conditional versus prescribed, planned versus completed;
- specificity: exact symptom, red flag, allergen, medicine, investigation, diagnosis, or body site;
- values: dose, frequency, duration, timing, threshold, amount, laterality, date;
- urgency and consequence: same-day, within hours, routine, emergency, call back, attend urgent care/A&E, call emergency services;
- rationale or safety role: why the advice matters, what deterioration it is meant to detect.

Common partial omissions to catch:
- A safety-net is reduced to “return if worse” or “red flags discussed” when the transcript gave specific danger signs or escalation routes.
- A triage baseline or serious-disease pertinent negative is mentioned only as a vague diagnostic aside, without clearly recording the patient-level negative that was established.
- Urgency is softened or lost: “needs sorting” is not the same as advice to avoid work, seek same-day/urgent assessment, or act before a stated event.
- Medication advice is changed by adding unsupported dose/frequency/duration, omitting stated dose/frequency/duration, or changing actual prescription into conditional advice or vice versa.
- A drug allergy or serious intolerance is omitted, hidden, or left only as a vague residual mention; allergies matter even when antibiotics or other drugs were only conditional or hypothetical.
- A threshold for escalation is omitted or altered, such as a number of episodes, time interval, fever threshold, bleeding, dehydration signs, neurological symptoms, non-blanching rash, or other condition-specific red flag.
- A diagnosis or differential is named but the reason, uncertainty, exclusion, or key supporting/against finding is dropped.

Classify problems by clinical effect:
- Critical: could plausibly cause unsafe treatment, delayed urgent care, wrong prescribing, allergic reaction, false reassurance, missed serious deterioration, or major misunderstanding of diagnosis/plan. This includes fabricated examinations/results, unsupported exact drug/dose instructions, omitted drug allergy, wrong escalation threshold, omitted urgent red-flag safety-net, altered urgent referral/review timing, or changed/negated high-risk findings.
- Supporting: materially weakens the clinical picture, differential, continuity, or rationale but is unlikely by itself to change immediate treatment or urgent safety.
- Peripheral: an established health-relevant detail is absent or slightly wrong but has little or no bearing on the current decision or safety. Penalise only lightly for these, and do not let peripheral trivia make an otherwise faithful note look unsafe.

Scoring should reflect separation:
- A note with no penalised differences should receive the top score.
- A note with a true critical fabrication, alteration, or omission should sit clearly below faithful notes even if the rest is good.
- A note with supporting omissions should sit below an equivalent complete note but above notes with critical safety errors.
- A note with only peripheral omissions should be only slightly below a complete note.
- If you are unsure whether a low-impact wording difference is a real omission or acceptable compression, prefer not to penalise. If you are unsure whether a high-risk safety, allergy, medication, red-flag, urgency, or pertinent-negative detail has been preserved, inspect the exact wording and penalise when the actionable content is not clear.

You are evaluating the clinical note against the consultation transcript it was generated from.

Your task is to order notes correctly, not to be generally harsh. A faithful, clinically acceptable summary should score above a note that loses, fabricates, or changes load-bearing clinical information. Penalise differences only when they would change, obscure, or materially weaken a clinician’s understanding of the consultation or the patient’s care.

Use the transcript as the source of truth. Before judging, identify the patient-specific facts established in the consultation that a careful clinician would rely on: presenting symptoms and duration; important positives and negatives; red flags and safety-net advice; diagnoses, differential diagnoses, impressions and rationale; medications, allergies and adverse reactions; pregnancy/contraception/smoking or other prescribing gatekeepers where relevant; past history and risk factors; examination or observed findings; test results; and the plan, including doses, timing, conditions, follow-up, referrals and what was actually agreed or completed.

Accept legitimate clinical summarisation. Do not penalise:
- paraphrase into clinical language or standard abbreviations;
- conventional concise normal/negative phrasing where it reasonably reflects what was assessed;
- omission of conversational colour, repetition, greetings, hesitation, or irrelevant process detail;
- recording a fact once rather than in every possible section;
- different ordering, headings, or wording;
- equivalent safety-net or management wording that preserves the key trigger, action and urgency;
- omission of details not established by the transcript.

Do penalise fabricated, altered, or omitted clinically significant content. This includes:
- asserting something as known when the transcript left it unknown, inaudible, unresolved, only proposed, or unconfirmed;
- converting uncertainty or a differential into a definite diagnosis, or weakening a clear diagnosis/impression into something noncommittal when that changes care;
- changing polarity, value, laterality, subject, timing, severity, dose, frequency, route, duration, or whether something is current/past;
- recording actions as completed when they were only discussed, or recording advice/understanding that was not given;
- omitting an established red flag, pertinent negative, prescribing safety factor, medication instruction, follow-up condition, or safety-net trigger.

Pay special attention to partial omissions. A surviving topic heading, keyword, or broad statement is not enough if the load-bearing part has been dropped. Ask: would a clinician reading only the note know the patient-specific fact, condition, rationale, or threshold established in the consultation? If not, treat it as missing even if the same general topic is mentioned elsewhere.

For partial coverage, check whether the note preserves:
- the patient-specific status, not just a generic checklist term;
- the clinician’s working impression and the reason for the plan, not merely absence of one severe alternative;
- conditional instructions, including when to start, stop, escalate, seek help, or follow up;
- safety-net specifics: the symptom/event, seriousness, urgency, and destination/action;
- prescribing-critical negatives and risk factors where they govern treatment choice;
- uncertainty, approximate timing, progression, or qualification when clinically relevant.

A fact may be counted as present if it is unambiguous anywhere in the note and clearly applies to this patient. Do not require the exact transcript wording. But do not infer missing patient-specific facts from vague phrases such as “red flags discussed,” “risk factors reviewed,” “safety-netted,” “no contraindications,” or a broad topic label unless the note actually records the clinically important content.

Severity guidance:
- Critical: likely to affect diagnosis, prescribing, escalation/urgency, safety-netting, contraindication assessment, allergy status, or major management decisions.
- Supporting: important for clinical reasoning, continuity, or interpreting the plan, but unlikely on its own to cause immediate unsafe care.
- Peripheral: true loss or inaccuracy with little or no expected effect on care.

Give high scores to notes that preserve all clinically load-bearing information despite concise wording. Lower the score clearly for any critical missing or wrong fact, even if the rest of the note is good. Use smaller deductions for supporting or peripheral losses. Do not pad the omissions list with nice-to-have improvements or speculative issues; include only differences you are actually penalising, stated concretely as the missing or wrong assertion and why it matters.
